### How the fitting works

`polyfit` only fits straight lines, so the exponential and power models are first **linearized** by taking logarithms, then converted back:

- **Linear:** `p = polyfit(Read, Flow, 1)`, giving `y = p(1)*x + p(2)`.
- **Power:** taking log10 of both sides of `y = b*x^m` gives `log10(y) = m*log10(x) + log10(b)`. Fitting a line to `log10(Wind)` and `log10(Power)` gives the slope `m` and intercept `log10(b)`, so `b = 10^p(2)`.
- **Exponential:** taking log10 of `y = b*10^(mx)` gives `log10(y) = m*x + log10(b)`. Fitting `Temp` against `log10(Life)` gives `m` and `b = 10^p(2)`.

`linspace` then builds 100 evenly spaced x values so the fitted curve plots smoothly.

### Limitations

The wind power data levels off at higher speeds, while a power-law curve keeps climbing, so the fit drifts above the data at the top end. A more realistic wind turbine model would flatten out there. The model choice here is also made by eye. A natural next step would be to compute R² for each candidate model and choose by number.

### Code

```matlab
clear; clc; close all;
load('emp3.mat')

%% Dataset 1: Flow vs Read
figure
subplot(1,3,1); plot(Read,Flow,'o'); title('Linear')
subplot(1,3,2); semilogy(Read,Flow,'o'); title('Semilog')
subplot(1,3,3); loglog(Read,Flow,'o'); title('Log-Log')
sgtitle('LEGO Flowmeter')

p = polyfit(Read,Flow,1);
x = linspace(min(Read),max(Read),100);
y = p(1)*x + p(2);

figure
plot(Read,Flow,'o',x,y,'-')
grid on
xlabel('Read'); ylabel('Flow')
title('Flow vs Read')
legend('Data','Best Fit')

%% Dataset 2: Power vs Wind
figure
subplot(1,3,1); plot(Wind,Power,'o'); title('Linear')
subplot(1,3,2); semilogy(Wind,Power,'o'); title('Semilog')
subplot(1,3,3); loglog(Wind,Power,'o'); title('Log-Log')
sgtitle('Wind Power')

p = polyfit(log10(Wind),log10(Power),1);
m = p(1);
b = 10^p(2);

x = linspace(min(Wind),max(Wind),100);
y = b*x.^m;

figure
plot(Wind,Power,'o',x,y,'-')
grid on
xlabel('Wind Speed (MPH)'); ylabel('Power (MW)')
title('Wind Power')
legend('Data','Best Fit')

%% Dataset 3: Life vs Temp
figure
subplot(1,3,1); plot(Temp,Life,'o'); title('Linear')
subplot(1,3,2); semilogy(Temp,Life,'o'); title('Semilog')
subplot(1,3,3); loglog(Temp,Life,'o'); title('Log-Log')
sgtitle('Bearing Life')

p = polyfit(Temp,log10(Life),1);
m = p(1);
b = 10^p(2);

x = linspace(min(Temp),max(Temp),100);
y = b*10.^(m*x);

figure
plot(Temp,Life,'o',x,y,'-')
grid on
xlabel('Temperature'); ylabel('Life')
title('Bearing Life')
legend('Data','Best Fit')
```

