### Code design notes

- `x = x(:); y = y(:);` forces both inputs to be column vectors, so the function works whether the user passes row or column data.
- An `if` check with `error(...)` stops the function if `x` and `y` have different lengths, which would otherwise cause a confusing failure later.
- The function returns the coefficients and R², and also draws the plot, so one call does the whole analysis.

### Limitations

The thermistor points curve slightly around the fitted line: the line sits below the data at the cool end, above it in the middle, and below again at the hot end. That pattern is typical of an NTC thermistor, whose resistance follows an exponential relationship with temperature. Linear is a reasonable approximation over this range (R² = 0.97), but fitting `ln(R)` against `T`, as in Activity 1, would likely fit better.

### Code

```matlab
clear; clc; close all;

temp = [25 30 35 40 45 50 55 60 65 70];
ohms = [5000 4200 3600 3000 2500 2000 1600 1300 1000 800];

[Z,R2] = leastSquares(temp,ohms);

disp('Z =')
disp(Z)
disp('R2 =')
disp(R2)

%% Function
function [Z,R2] = leastSquares(x,y)

x = x(:);
y = y(:);

if length(x) ~= length(y)
    error('x and y must be the same length')
end

n = length(x);

m = (n*sum(x.*y)-sum(x)*sum(y)) / ...
    (n*sum(x.^2)-(sum(x))^2);

b = mean(y)-m*mean(x);

yhat = m*x+b;

R2 = 1-sum((y-yhat).^2)/sum((y-mean(y)).^2);

Z = [b m];

xfit = linspace(min(x),max(x),100);
yfit = m*xfit+b;

figure
plot(x,y,'o',xfit,yfit,'-')
grid on
xlabel('Temperature (C)')
ylabel('Resistance (Ohms)')
title('Thermistor Linear Fit')
legend('Data','Best Fit')

end
```

